You are an expert in analyzing a subgraph sampled from a knowledge graph and generating natural language questions,
along with their corresponding SPARQL queries.

You will be given a subgraph description loaded from a YAML file, potentially accompanied by knowledge graph metadata
(title and description).

Notes on the YAML structure (generic, do not assume any specific KG):
- Each subgraph includes: subgraph_id, support, prefixes, types, attributes, relationships, and instances.
- prefixes maps prefix → namespace URI; types maps type display name → class CURIE.
- Important: the YAML prefixes are auto-generated shorthand and must not be copied as-is. Expand CURIEs using those
namespace URIs, then declare more meaningful SPARQL prefixes yourself.
- attributes maps a type name to compact property lines: "--name (curie)--> datatype", joined by " | ".
- relationships lists schema edges as "TypeA --predicateCURIE--> TypeB".
- instances maps 1..n grounded examples of the subgraph pattern
- A name ending in ^ came from rdfs:label as opposed to being extracted from URI. An arrow "-->*" marks an optional edge
or attribute (below the subgraph's support threshold).

### Task:
First, assess whether the subgraph allows for meaningful questions. If the subgraph is too trivial, lacks interesting
relationships (e.g., graph metadata rather than actual knowledge), or cannot support useful, meaningful questions, set
is_meaningful to false and return an empty questions list. If it at least has some meaningful concepts and natural
domain terms to elicit a question of decent or high utility, set is_meaningful to true.

If is_meaningful is true, generate 2-4 question-query pairs as a JSON array where each item has:
- text_query: the natural language question
- sparql_query: the corresponding SPARQL query

### Requirements:

#### A. Task Information
1. Generate 2-4 question-query pairs depending on your assessment of the subgraph's richness. Try to achieve high
coverage of all the meaningful aspects of the subgraph across the question-query pairs.
{% if focus_entity_qa %}
2. Great questions are often centered on interesting entities in the graph (entities with a proper natural language
name/label or specific real-world referent — not blank nodes or anonymous instances). All of the questions you produce
must be entity-centric. If no interesting entities are available, set is_meaningful to false instead.
3. You must make the 2-4 questions diverse in style (what, who, whom, whose, when, which, yes/no (ASK) questions,
how-compounds, aggregate statistics, superlatives/comparatives, etc.) and in complexity, while ultimately maintaining
the quality, meaningfulness, and utility of the questions.
{% else %}
2. Whenever there is an interesting entity in the graph (one with a proper natural language nam/labele or specific
real-world referent), it's good to have a question or two specifically about it.
3. You must make the 2-4 questions diverse in style (what, who, whom, whose, when, which, yes/no (ASK) questions,
how-compounds, aggregate statistics, superlatives/comparatives, etc.) and in complexity, while ultimately maintaining
the quality, meaningfulness, and utility of the questions.
{% endif %}


#### B. Natural Language Question Quality:
4. Questions must be grounded exclusively in the meaningful classes, entities, relationships, and attributes present in
the YAML subgraph. Never use knowledge-graph jargon (generic properties, types, etc.), mention URIs/prefixes/IDs, or
reference
the YAML/subgraph in the natural language question — each question and query must be fully self-contained.
5. The phrasing of each natural language question should always be natural, fluent, and not awkward or stilted. Each
question should always end with a question mark. If a term drawn from the graph reads awkwardly, and you are confident
it can be rephrased naturally without sacrificing accuracy, do so.
6. Questions must be definitively answerable and should avoid ontology/schema/documentation questions. Focus on
questions that are interesting and meaningful (avoid meaningless questions like "what/how many entities are linked
to…"/other uninformative patterns).
7. Only write questions that would be of high-utility to domain experts. The questions will be reviewed by domain
experts and need to have sufficient value and meaning.


#### C. SPARQL Accuracy & Question-Query Alignment:
8. SPARQL queries must be standalone and executable against a SPARQL endpoint containing the full graph. Declare
meaningful prefixes in the query based on the namespace URIs in the YAML; do not reuse the auto-generated YAML prefix
names.
9. Use proper SPARQL syntax to generate valid queries. The SPARQL query must produce exactly the results expected by the
text query. Before finalizing each pair, verify: does the SPARQL query include every constraint stated in the question,
and does it avoid any constraints not stated in the question?
10. If the question does not have one answer/result, you may or may not use LIMIT in SELECT queries (e.g., LIMIT 100) to
avoid too many results. BUT MAKE SURE TO REFLECT THIS IN THE TEXT QUERY!

### Crucial Notes:
The ultimate goal is to produce high-quality, diverse, and highly meaningful questions, together with their
corresponding accurate SPARQL queries, that can be executed against the full knowledge graph.

An expert in the knowledge graph should be able to reproduce the same SPARQL query when given only the text question.
- You will be rewarded when you generate high utility questions that an expert in the domain would ask, where the SPARQL
query is perfectly aligned with the question and accurately answers it.
- You will be penalized if the generated SPARQL query and the text question do not properly align (e.g., the SPARQL
query includes constraints not requested in the question, or omits ones that were requested).
- You will also be highly penalized if the generated SPARQL query incorrectly answers the text question or provides no
answer when one exists.
- You will be penalized if your question styles are not diverse enough, or if questions are of low quality or are
awkwardly phrased.
- Flagging the subgraph as not meaningful when it's hard to generate high-quality and high utility questions is better
than generating low-quality ones.
- Generating fewer high-quality questions that a DOMAIN EXPERT may ask is MUCH better than generating more low-quality
questions. Quality over quantity.